    {
      "id": "UgzsWloJ6FIcYPyQY5d4AaABAg",
      "author": "@g.pattonhughes5991",
      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/ytc/AIdro_kFWrglk-ru1Je85IxqX-jYa2FGFuVxI-Y4bpXKYFtODg=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "First thing is we need to thank these people; those enduring these hellish conditions, for their service. They are out there getting sick, dying early cleaning up our massive and increasingly toxic waste so we can enjoy the good life with our streaming services and million dollar candy bars.  \n\nIndeed, shouldn't someone be engaged in an effort to replace the 'garbage cities' - the neighborhoods build on or around major dumps that, in some cases, do an amazing job at recycling - with some kind of humanoid robot that is 'hardened' against the toxins and challenges of recycling our garbage. \n\nIf you think just a bit further down the logic chain, you'll soon realize that AI and robotics will likely be employed more diligently on behalf the oligarchs if they invest in solving other problems like exploiting tax loopholes or manufacturing more widgets, cheaper and faster - not to mention the waste-stream champion, disposable packaging.  AI  and those using it to enhance business have no interest at all in minimizing the waste in the waste-stream as their entire reason for being is selling more product. \n\nThat said, if the first humanoid robots were designed to safely recycle toxic wastes or work to solve key elements in recycling, it would be seen as a more selfless effort by those in that industry and would like enhance the reputation of the robotic maker making that effort.",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-04T00:12:56Z",
      "replyCount": 0,
      "replies": []
    },
